ROWS = [
dict(id='T147',
 x="Moved on 12 Nov 2026 to the list of open problems in mathematics generally (06_math_research/top200_general_2026_11_10, seed_from_probability.tsv): Sidorenko's conjecture: extremal graph theory.",
 d='COMB',
 t="Sidorenko's conjecture",
 s='For every bipartite graph H and every graph G, the homomorphism densities satisfy t(H, G) ≥ t(K_2, G)^{e(H)}; more generally t(H, W) ≥ (∫W)^{e(H)} for every graphon W. In probabilistic form: if the vertices of H are mapped independently and uniformly at random to vertices of G, the probability that every edge of H lands on an edge of G is at least p^{e(H)}, where p = 2e(G)/|V(G)|² is the edge density of G.',
 c='Bipartiteness is necessary: an odd cycle has density 0 in a bipartite G of positive edge density. Sidorenko stated the conjecture as a correlation inequality for integrals of products of a nonnegative kernel along the edges of H; Erdős and Simonovits made an equivalent conjecture in graph language (Simonovits 1984).',
 src='A. F. Sidorenko, A correlation inequality for bipartite graphs, Graphs Combin. 9 (1993) 201–204; M. Simonovits, Extremal graph problems, degenerate extremal problems, and supersaturated graphs, in Progress in Graph Theory (Waterloo, 1982), Academic Press, Toronto (1984) 419–437',
 why='It says that among all graphs of a given edge density the random graph has the fewest copies of every bipartite graph, a correlation inequality for products of a kernel along the edges of a bipartite graph. Its strengthening, the forcing conjecture of Skokan and Thoma, would characterise quasirandom graphs by the count of any bipartite graph with a cycle (Skokan–Thoma, Graphs Combin. 20 (2004) 255–262); it is a central question about extremal problems for graph limits.',
 st='Proved for trees and even cycles, complete bipartite graphs, hypercubes (Hatami 2010), bipartite graphs with a vertex joined to every vertex of the other side (Conlon–Fox–Sudakov, Geom. Funct. Anal. 20 (2010)), and wider classes closed under certain gluing operations (Szegedy; Kim–Lee–Lee, Trans. Amer. Math. Soc. 368 (2016); Conlon–Kim–Lee–Lee, J. Lond. Math. Soc. 98 (2018)). The smallest open case is usually given as K_{5,5} with a Hamiltonian cycle removed. Zhao (arXiv:2606.23018, arXiv:2607.02260, 2026) proves the inequality for special classes of host kernels, such as two-sided correlation kernels on finite groups. Open.'),
dict(id='T148',
 x='Moved on 12 Nov 2026 to the list of open problems in mathematics generally (06_math_research/top200_general_2026_11_10, seed_from_probability.tsv): Union-closed sets conjecture: combinatorics.',
 d='COMB',
 t="Frankl's union-closed sets conjecture",
 s='If F is a finite family of subsets of a finite set, closed under unions and containing a nonempty set, then some element belongs to at least half of the members of F. Equivalently, if A is a uniformly random member of F, then P(x ∈ A) ≥ 1/2 for some element x.',
 c='The power set shows that 1/2 cannot be improved. Since 2022 the probabilistic form has been attacked with entropy inequalities for two independent random members of F.',
 src='Usually attributed to P. Frankl (1979); stated in print by D. Duffus in I. Rival (ed.), Graphs and Order, NATO ASI Series C 147, Reidel, Dordrecht (1985), p. 525; survey: H. Bruhn and O. Schaudt, The journey of the union-closed sets conjecture, Graphs Combin. 31 (2015) 2043–2074',
 why="It is one of the best-known elementary questions in combinatorics, and Gilmer's entropy argument turned it into a question about entropy and couplings of random sets; the best constants now come from information inequalities, and the gap between them and 1/2 measures how far those methods reach.",
 st='Gilmer (arXiv:2211.09055, 2022) proved that some element lies in at least a 0.01 fraction of the members, by an entropy argument; Alweiss–Huang–Sellke, Chase–Lovett and Sawin raised the fraction to (3 − √5)/2 ≈ 0.382, and later work went slightly beyond. The best bound is now about 0.38289 (Jiang, arXiv:2609.08291, computer-assisted; Costa–Sadhu, arXiv:2610.02295, 0.3828852549…). The conjecture is known for small ground sets and small families, and for families containing at least two thirds of all subsets of the ground set (Balla–Bollobás–Eccles, J. Combin. Theory Ser. A 120 (2013)). Open.'),
dict(id='T149',
 x='Moved on 12 Nov 2026 to the list of open problems in mathematics generally (06_math_research/top200_general_2026_11_10, seed_from_probability.tsv): Asymptotics of R(3,k): combinatorics.',
 d='RG',
 t='The asymptotics of the Ramsey number R(3,k)',
 s='The Ramsey number R(3,k), the least n such that every graph on n vertices contains a triangle or an independent set of k vertices, satisfies R(3,k) = (1/2 + o(1)) k²/log k as k → ∞.',
 c='Erdős asked for an asymptotic formula for R(3,k). The bounds are now (1/2 + o(1)) k²/log k ≤ R(3,k) ≤ (1 + o(1)) k²/log k, so the question is the constant. The constant 1/2 was conjectured by Campos, Jenssen, Michelen and Sahasrabudhe and is recorded as Conjecture 1.1 by Hefty, Horn, King and Pfender; an earlier conjecture that the triangle-free process is optimal, with constant 1/4, was disproved in 2025.',
 src='P. Erdős, Erdős problem #165 (erdosproblems.com/165); J. H. Kim, The Ramsey number R(3,t) has order of magnitude t²/log t, Random Structures Algorithms 7 (1995) 173–207; R. Hefty, P. Horn, D. King and F. Pfender, arXiv:2510.19718 (2025)',
 loc='Hefty–Horn–King–Pfender, arXiv:2510.19718, Conjecture 1.1 (the constant 1/2) and Theorem 1.2 (the lower bound)',
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.19718',
 why="R(3,k) is the founding example of the probabilistic method in Ramsey theory: its lower bounds come from random greedy processes (the semi-random nibble and the triangle-free process) and its upper bound from Shearer's bound on the independence number of triangle-free graphs. Matching the constants would show which random construction is optimal.",
 st='Order of magnitude: Ajtai–Komlós–Szemerédi (1980) above and Kim (1995) below. Shearer (1983) gives the upper constant 1. Bohman–Keevash and Fiz Pontiveros–Griffiths–Morris (2013) gave the lower constant 1/4 through the triangle-free process; Campos, Jenssen, Michelen and Sahasrabudhe (arXiv:2505.13371, 2025) gave 1/3, disproving the conjecture that 1/4 is the truth; Hefty, Horn, King and Pfender (arXiv:2510.19718, 2025) gave 1/2. The constant is open.'),
dict(id="T150", d="SM", t="Temperature chaos in the Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model",
 s="For the SK model H_N(σ) = N^{−1/2} Σ_{i<j} g_ij σ_i σ_j with iid standard Gaussian couplings and no external field, fix 1 < β < β′. Sample σ from the Gibbs measure at inverse temperature β and τ independently from the Gibbs measure at β′, both with the same couplings. Then the overlap R(σ, τ) = N^{−1} Σ_i σ_i τ_i tends to 0 in probability as N → ∞.",
 c="With an external field h ≠ 0 the overlap should concentrate at a constant depending on β and β′ instead. Physics predicts that temperature chaos in the SK model is very weak, appearing only at high order in perturbation theory (Rizzo–Crisanti, Phys. Rev. Lett. 90 (2003) 137201); the proofs so far use the extra structure of models with many p-spin terms.",
 src="A. J. Bray and M. A. Moore, Chaotic nature of the spin-glass phase, Phys. Rev. Lett. 58 (1987) 57–60; D. Panchenko, Chaos in temperature in generic 2p-spin models, Comm. Math. Phys. 346 (2016), arXiv:1502.03801",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1502.03801",
 why="Chaos, the extreme sensitivity of the low-temperature Gibbs measure to small changes of the parameters, is a defining feature of the spin-glass phase in the Parisi picture and is invoked to explain rejuvenation and memory in experiments; for temperature it means that the pure states at two different low temperatures are unrelated.",
 st="Proved (claimed): Aronow and Lopatto (arXiv:2609.17442, September 2026), in identifying the limiting overlap structure of mixed even p-spin models, prove temperature chaos for the zero-field SK model at every pair of distinct nonnegative inverse temperatures (abstract read 14 Oct; the SK case is Corollary 9.2). Before that: disorder chaos was proved for the SK model, without an external field (Chatterjee, arXiv:0907.3381, 2009) and with one (Chen, Ann. Probab. 41 (2013)); temperature chaos was proved for generic mixed even p-spin models that contain sufficiently many p-spin terms (Panchenko, arXiv:1502.03801) and for some spherical mixed models (Chen–Panchenko, arXiv:1608.02478), and it fails for spherical pure p-spin models with p ≥ 3 (Ben Arous–Subag–Zeitouni, arXiv:1804.10573).",
 x="SOLVED: Aronow and Lopatto (arXiv:2609.17442, 15 Sep 2026) prove temperature chaos for the zero-field SK model at every pair of distinct nonnegative inverse temperatures (abstract read 14 Oct; w673 opened Corollary 9.2). A preprint claim, not independently checked."),
dict(id="T151", d="SM", t="Zamolodchikov's E8 particle spectrum for the critical Ising model in a magnetic field",
 s="Take the nearest-neighbour Ising model on aZ² at its critical temperature with external field h a^{15/8}, h > 0, and let a → 0. The massive field theory obtained in the limit has eight particles, with masses proportional to the components of the positive (Perron–Frobenius) eigenvector of the adjacency matrix of the E8 Dynkin diagram, equivalently the eigenvector of the E8 Cartan matrix for its smallest eigenvalue; in particular m_2/m_1 = 2 cos(π/5), the golden ratio, and three of the eight masses lie below the two-particle threshold 2m_1.",
 c="In probabilistic terms the masses are exponential decay rates of truncated correlations of the scaling limit Φ^h of the magnetization field, and the statement concerns the spectrum of the relativistic quantum field theory reconstructed from Φ^h (Osterwalder–Schrader); Camia, Jiang and Newman describe the masses through a stationary Gaussian process built from Φ^h. In the form they pose it, the truncated two-point function of Φ^h along a line decays as a sum of three exponentials with rates m_1, m_2, m_3 below the threshold 2m_1, with the E8 ratios, plus a continuum; a positive mass gap alone does not give even the first isolated mass.",
 loc="Camia–Jiang–Newman, arXiv:2111.05134, Section 1.2, equation (3)",
 src="A. B. Zamolodchikov, Integrals of motion and S-matrix of the (scaled) T = T_c Ising model with magnetic field, Int. J. Mod. Phys. A 4 (1989) 4235–4248; F. Camia, J. Jiang and C. M. Newman, The Gaussian process for particle masses in the near-critical Ising model, arXiv:2111.05134 (2021)",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.05134",
 why="It is the most celebrated prediction of integrable field theory for a lattice model of probability: a hidden E8 structure in the simplest perturbation of the critical Ising model, with the golden ratio as a mass ratio, later seen in neutron scattering on cobalt niobate (Coldea et al., Science 327 (2010)). A proof would derive an interacting massive particle spectrum from a lattice model.",
 st="The scaling limits of the critical magnetization field and of the near-critical model in a field exist (Camia–Garban–Newman, Ann. Probab. 43 (2015); Ann. Inst. Henri Poincaré Probab. Stat. 52 (2016)); the near-critical limit has exponentially decaying correlations, so a positive lowest mass (Camia–Jiang–Newman, Comm. Pure Appl. Math. 73 (2020)); and the masses are tied to a Gaussian process recovered from the lattice magnetization along a line (Camia–Jiang–Newman, arXiv:2111.05134). A second particle, the mass ratios and the E8 structure are open."),
dict(id='T152',
 x='Moved on 12 Nov 2026 to the list of open problems in mathematics generally (06_math_research/top200_general_2026_11_10, seed_from_probability.tsv): Spectral gap on SU(2): group theory and number theory.',
 d='RW',
 t='Spectral gap for random walks on SU(2) (Gamburd–Jakobson–Sarnak)',
 s='For every n ≥ 2 and Haar-almost every n-tuple (g_1, …, g_n) in SU(2), the averaging operator T f(x) = (2n)^{−1} Σ_i (f(g_i x) + f(g_i^{−1} x)) on L²(SU(2)) has norm strictly less than 1 on the functions of mean zero. Equivalently, for every f in L²(SU(2)) the averages T^k f converge to the Haar mean of f exponentially fast in L²; the law of the walk at time k is atomic, so the equidistribution is in this weak sense, not in total variation.',
 c='The stronger statement, that every finite symmetric set generating a dense subgroup of SU(2) has a spectral gap, is also open; it is the spectral gap conjecture named in the title of the Fisher paper (Int. Math. Res. Not. 2006). Density is needed: a set generating a finite group has no gap.',
 src='A. Gamburd, D. Jakobson and P. Sarnak, Spectra of elements in the group ring of SU(2), J. Eur. Math. Soc. 1 (1999) 51–85',
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.17869',
 why='A spectral gap is the compact-group analogue of an expander: random words in a few rotations fill SU(2) at the optimal exponential rate. It governs how efficiently a finite universal set of single-qubit gates approximates every gate, and the cases proved so far needed the sum–product machinery of additive combinatorics.',
 st='Proved for elements with algebraic entries (Bourgain–Gamburd, Invent. Math. 171 (2008)), extended to SU(d) (Bourgain–Gamburd, arXiv:1108.6264) and to compact simple Lie groups (Benoist–de Saxcé, Invent. Math. 205 (2016)), always under an algebraicity or Diophantine condition. Fisher (Out(F_n) and the spectral gap conjecture, Int. Math. Res. Not. 2006) proved a zero–one law for n ≥ 3, so that either almost every n-tuple has a gap or almost none does; Pikhurko and Sakamoto (arXiv:2603.17869, 2026) proved the zero–one law for n = 2. The almost-every statement is open.'),
dict(id="T153", d="SM", t="Critical exponents and scaling limit of the three-dimensional self-avoiding walk",
 s="For the uniformly distributed n-step self-avoiding walk ω on Z³ started at the origin, there are exponents ν and γ such that E|ω(n)|² = n^{2ν + o(1)} and c_n = μ^n n^{γ − 1 + o(1)}, where c_n is the number of such walks and μ the connective constant; moreover there is a normalisation a_n = n^{ν + o(1)} such that the linearly interpolated path t ↦ ω(nt)/a_n converges in distribution, uniformly on compact time intervals, to a nondegenerate continuum random curve.",
 c="Numerically ν = 0.58759700(40) (Clisby–Dünweg, Phys. Rev. E 94 (2016) 052102) and γ = 1.15695300(95) (Clisby, J. Phys. A 50 (2017) 264003), close to but not equal to Flory's ν = 3/5. The continuum limit has no candidate description: unlike two dimensions (SLE(8/3), T003) there is no conformal-invariance prediction to aim at. T015 asks for the inequality E|ω(n)|² ≥ c n, a different statement that does not follow from the existence of ν.",
 src="N. Madras and G. Slade, The Self-Avoiding Walk, Birkhäuser (1993); R. Bauerschmidt, H. Duminil-Copin, J. Goodman and G. Slade, Lectures on self-avoiding walks, arXiv:1206.2092 (2012)",
 loc="Bauerschmidt–Duminil-Copin–Goodman–Slade, arXiv:1206.2092, Section 1.6.3",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.2092",
 why="The self-avoiding walk is the standard model of a linear polymer in a good solvent, and three dimensions is the physical case. Proving that its critical exponents exist below dimension four is one of the central open problems of the subject.",
 st="For d ≥ 5 the walk is diffusive with a Brownian scaling limit (Hara–Slade 1992). In three dimensions it is known that the walk is subballistic, E|ω(n)|² = o(n²) (Duminil-Copin–Hammond, Comm. Math. Phys. 324 (2013)), and the counting bound c_n ≤ μ^n exp(O(n^{2/5} log n)) goes back to Kesten (On the number of self-avoiding walks. II, J. Math. Phys. 5 (1964) 1128–1137); the e^{o(√n)} improvement of the Hammersley–Welsh bound by Hutchcroft (arXiv:1708.09460) matters in two dimensions. The existence of ν and γ, and any scaling limit, are open."),
dict(id="T154", d="SM", t="Logarithmic corrections at the critical point of the four-dimensional Ising model",
 s="For the nearest-neighbour Ising model on Z⁴, as β ↑ β_c the susceptibility, the correlation length (the inverse exponential decay rate of the two-point function) and the specific heat satisfy χ(β) ~ A t^{−1} |log t|^{1/3}, ξ(β) ~ B t^{−1/2} |log t|^{1/6} and C(β) ~ D |log t|^{1/3}, where t = β_c − β; at β_c the two-point function decays like c|x|^{−2}, without a logarithmic factor.",
 c="These are the one-component cases of the renormalisation-group predictions for the four-dimensional n-component |φ|⁴ model, whose logarithm exponents are (n + 2)/(n + 8), (n + 2)/(2(n + 8)) and (4 − n)/(n + 8). The self-avoiding walk is the case n = 0 (T043).",
 src="F. J. Wegner and E. K. Riedel, Logarithmic corrections to the molecular-field behavior of critical and tricritical systems, Phys. Rev. B 7 (1973) 248–256; M. Aizenman and H. Duminil-Copin, Marginal triviality of the scaling limits of critical 4D Ising and φ⁴₄ models, Ann. of Math. 194 (2021), arXiv:1912.07973",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.07973",
 why="Dimension four is the upper critical dimension of the Ising model, where mean-field behaviour holds up to logarithms; the logarithms are the visible trace of the marginal triviality of φ⁴₄, and deriving them for the lattice Ising model itself, rather than for a weakly coupled model, would be a renormalisation-group computation at strong coupling.",
 st="The critical scaling limit in four dimensions is Gaussian (Aizenman–Duminil-Copin 2021), and mean-field bounds hold up to logarithmic factors; neither gives the logarithms. The predicted logarithms are proved for the weakly coupled n-component |φ|⁴ model (Hara–Tasaki, J. Stat. Phys. 47 (1987); Bauerschmidt–Brydges–Slade, J. Stat. Phys. 157 (2014)) and for the weakly self-avoiding walk, by rigorous renormalisation group; for the nearest-neighbour Ising model they are open. Simulations disagree about the specific heat: Lundow and Markström (arXiv:2209.05292) conclude that it stays bounded at criticality and that the Ising and φ⁴ models are in different classes, while Li, Xiao, Zhou and Fang (arXiv:2408.15230) report finite-size evidence for the predicted logarithmic corrections."),
dict(id="T155", d="SM", t="Absence of a spin-glass phase in the two-dimensional Edwards–Anderson model",
 s="For the Edwards–Anderson model on Z² with Hamiltonian H(σ) = −Σ_{x∼y} J_xy σ_x σ_y, iid standard Gaussian couplings J_xy and no external field, at every inverse temperature β < ∞ there is, for almost every realisation of the couplings, a unique infinite-volume Gibbs measure, and its correlations decay exponentially.",
 c="This is the expected picture of a transition only at zero temperature, with a correlation length that diverges as the temperature tends to 0. Uniqueness is the strong form; it implies that the Edwards–Anderson order parameter vanishes, since a unique Gibbs measure is invariant under the global spin flip. Ground states (β = ∞) are a different question (T052, retired: a claimed proof for the periodic-boundary metastate).",
 src="C. M. Newman and D. L. Stein, Spin Glasses and Complexity, Princeton University Press (2013); A. Bovier, Statistical Mechanics of Disordered Systems, Cambridge University Press (2006)",
 why="Two dimensions is the one finite dimension in which the physics of short-range spin glasses is settled numerically, with no transition at positive temperature; a proof would separate the two-dimensional model rigorously from the three-dimensional one, where a positive-temperature transition is expected (T025).",
 st="Uniqueness is known at high temperature and in one dimension. In two dimensions the results concern ground states: uniqueness of the ground-state pair on the half-plane (Arguin–Damron–Newman–Stein, Comm. Math. Phys. 300 (2010)), and Newman and Stein (arXiv:2510.27507v2, revised 2026) claim that in two dimensions the metastate generated by, for example, periodic boundary conditions is unique and supported on a single pair of spin-reversed ground states (T052). No uniqueness or decay result is known at low positive temperature. Open.",
 n="Fame borderline (my judgment)."),
dict(id="T156", d="GEOM", t="The value of the Beardwood–Halton–Hammersley constant",
 s="If X_1, X_2, … are independent uniform points in the unit square and L_n is the length of the shortest closed tour through X_1, …, X_n, then L_n/√n → β almost surely (Beardwood, Halton and Hammersley 1959). Determine β; numerically β ≈ 0.7124.",
 c="No closed form for β is known or conjectured, so the row asks for the value, or for any description of β beyond simulation. In dimension d the analogous constants are predicted to satisfy β_d ~ √(d/(2πe)) as d → ∞ (Percus–Martin, Phys. Rev. Lett. 76 (1996) 1188–1191).",
 src="J. Beardwood, J. H. Halton and J. M. Hammersley, The shortest path through many points, Proc. Cambridge Philos. Soc. 55 (1959) 299–327; J. M. Steele, Probability Theory and Combinatorial Optimization, SIAM (1997)",
 why="It is the founding theorem of the probabilistic analysis of combinatorial optimisation (Karp's partitioning algorithm for the travelling salesman problem rests on it), and its constant has resisted every method; the value would require understanding the local structure of optimal tours, which statistical physicists treat with the cavity method.",
 st="Beardwood, Halton and Hammersley proved 0.625 ≤ β ≤ 0.92116; later work (Steinerberger, Adv. Appl. Probab. 47 (2015); Gaudio–Jaillet, Oper. Res. Lett. 48 (2020) for the lower bound) brought this to 0.6277 ≤ β ≤ 0.90367, the best bounds before 2026 as stated in the abstract of arXiv:2610.02809, and Dong and Cao (arXiv:2610.02809, October 2026) prove 0.6421 ≤ β ≤ 0.8810, with 0.6536 ≤ β ≤ 0.8749 holding with probability at least 1 − 2·10⁻⁴ over their importance-sampling procedure (a randomized bound, not a deterministic one). Simulations give β ≈ 0.712 (Johnson–McGeoch–Rothberg 1996: 0.7124; Percus–Martin 1996: 0.7120). Open.",
 n="Fame borderline (my judgment): a famous constant rather than a conjecture."),
dict(id='T157',
 x='Moved on 12 Nov 2026 to the list of open problems in mathematics generally (06_math_research/top200_general_2026_11_10, seed_from_probability.tsv): Henon attractor at the classical parameters: smooth dynamics.',
 d='DYN',
 t='The strange attractor of the Hénon map at the classical parameters',
 s='The Hénon map H(x, y) = (1 − a x² + y, b x) with a = 1.4, b = 0.3 has a strange attractor carrying a unique SRB (physical) measure μ: for Lebesgue-almost every initial point z in its basin the empirical measures (1/n) Σ_{k<n} δ_{H^k(z)} converge to μ, and μ has a positive Lyapunov exponent.',
 c='The competing possibility usually discussed is that the observed attractor is a stable periodic orbit of very long period, which simulations cannot rule out; for the same reason the question is not settled by computation.',
 src='M. Hénon, A two-dimensional mapping with a strange attractor, Comm. Math. Phys. 50 (1976) 69–77; M. Benedicks and L. Carleson, The dynamics of the Hénon map, Ann. of Math. 133 (1991) 73–169',
 why='The Hénon map is the standard example of a strange attractor in a dissipative system, and its description by an SRB measure is the model case of the programme, going back to Sinai, Ruelle and Bowen, that describes chaotic dynamics by invariant probability measures and their limit theorems.',
 st="Benedicks and Carleson proved that for small b and a positive-measure set of a near 2 the map has a strange attractor, and Benedicks and Young (1993) constructed SRB measures for those parameters; Wang and Young extended the theory to wider classes of rank-one attractors (Ann. of Math. 167 (2008)) (Benedicks–Young, Invent. Math. 112 (1993) 541–576). Benedicks–Carleson parameters are approximated by parameters with homoclinic tangencies (Ures, Ergodic Theory Dynam. Systems 15 (1995)), hence by parameters with periodic sinks through Newhouse's theory, so the classical values cannot be reached by perturbation. Computer-assisted work at the classical parameters locates a trapping region and many periodic orbits (Galias–Tucker, Chaos 25 (2015)) without deciding the question. Open.",
 n='Fame as a probability problem borderline (my judgment): a problem of smooth ergodic theory.'),
dict(id='T158',
 x="Moved on 12 Nov 2026 to the list of open problems in mathematics generally (06_math_research/top200_general_2026_11_10, seed_from_probability.tsv): Amenability of Thompson's group F: group theory.",
 d='RW',
 t="Amenability of Thompson's group F (Kesten's criterion)",
 s="Let F = ⟨x_0, x_1 | [x_0 x_1^{−1}, x_0^{−1} x_1 x_0], [x_0 x_1^{−1}, x_0^{−2} x_1 x_0²]⟩ be Richard Thompson's group, and p_{2n} the probability that simple random walk on F with steps x_0^{±1}, x_1^{±1} is at the identity at time 2n. Decide whether p_{2n}^{1/(2n)} → 1, that is, whether the spectral radius of the walk is 1; by Kesten's theorem this holds if and only if F is amenable.",
 c="The question is usually posed in group-theoretic language (Geoghegan 1979; Cannon–Floyd–Parry, Enseign. Math. 42 (1996)); it is on this list because Kesten's criterion makes it a question about return probabilities of a random walk, and numerical attacks on it are random-walk computations. Neither answer is proved.",
 src="R. Geoghegan, question of 1979 recorded in J. W. Cannon, W. J. Floyd and W. R. Parry, Introductory notes on Richard Thompson's groups, Enseign. Math. (2) 42 (1996) 215–256; H. Kesten, Symmetric random walks on groups, Trans. Amer. Math. Soc. 92 (1959) 336–354; V. Guba, Amenability problem for Thompson's group F: state of the art, arXiv:2305.07113",
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.07113',
 why='F has no free subgroups, so if it is not amenable it is a natural finitely presented counterexample to the von Neumann–Day problem, far simpler than the known ones; if it is amenable it is amenable without being elementary amenable. As a random-walk problem it asks for the exponential decay rate of return probabilities on one explicit Cayley graph.',
 st='F has no free subgroup (Brin–Squier, Invent. Math. 79 (1985)) and is not elementary amenable (Chou, Illinois J. Math. 24 (1980)). Estimates of the cogrowth and of the norm of the Markov operator, partly computer-assisted (Elder–Rechnitzer–Janse van Rensburg, Exp. Math. 24 (2015); Haagerup–Haagerup–Ramirez-Solano, Internat. J. Algebra Comput. 25 (2015)), and density estimates for finite subgraphs of the Cayley graph of F (Guba, arXiv:2305.07113, a survey of density estimates for finite subgraphs of Cayley graphs of F) have not decided the question. Open.',
 n='Fame as a probability problem borderline (my judgment): a group-theory problem with a random-walk formulation.'),
]
